24913	{"artifactId":"PLAN-93081bff91ae5108-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-PLAN-93081bff91ae5108-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:controller","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:16:31.843Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"plan","stage":"plan","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"q1","ref":"t5 / c16","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The range-base stamp has exactly ONE site, approveArtifactByJsonPath in src/workflow/gates.ts, and that site is GENERIC across every artifact kind and SYNCHRONOUS — which is why t5 must gate the stamp on meta.workflow rather than writing it unconditionally, and must not use an await-based HEAD read. It builds nextMeta by spreading the prior meta.","evidence":"Confirmed on all three anchors. gates.ts:499 `export function approveArtifactByJsonPath(jsonPath: string, opts?: ...)` — note `export function`, not async, so the SYNCHRONOUS half is verified from the signature itself. gates.ts:497 carries the comment 'Works generically for any workflow — the artifact's JSON path is passed in verbatim', verifying the GENERIC half verbatim. gates.ts:537 is `const nextMeta = { ...artifact.meta, approvedAt, ...(reviewOverride !== undefined ? { reviewOverride } : {}) };`, verifying the spread. Only one src/ hit for the declaration pattern, so the one-site claim holds.","action":"none — verified sound. Both constraints t5 depends on (gate on meta.workflow; no await) rest on verified properties of the real function rather than on assumption.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q2","ref":"t5 / c17","kind":"external-contract","severity":"LOW","premise":"revParse hard-codes `--short`, so it CANNOT supply the full 40-hex sha t5 requires for a persisted range base. This is the premise behind t5's instruction to run `git rev-parse HEAD` directly via execFileSync instead, and not to widen revParse.","evidence":"Confirmed decisively, and this is the claim that justifies t5's most specific instruction. helpers.ts:40 declares `export async function revParse(cwd: string, ref: string): Promise<string>` and :41 is `const res = await runShell(['git', 'rev-parse', '--short', ref], { cwd, timeoutMs: 5_000 });` — `--short` is hard-coded in the argv array with no parameter controlling it. Exactly ONE src/ hit for that argv pattern. So revParse genuinely cannot return a full sha, and t5's direction to call `git rev-parse HEAD` separately rather than widening revParse is grounded.","action":"none — verified sound. The s3 critique that produced this instruction was correct: without it the build would have reached for revParse and silently persisted an abbreviation.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q3","ref":"t5 / c16","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"A non-test synchronous execFileSync precedent already exists inside src/workflow/, which is what makes t5's choice of a sync git call an existing idiom rather than a new one. The two sites are migrate-docs-tree.ts and tracker/github.ts.","evidence":"Both read anchors resolve (migrate-docs-tree.ts:317, tracker/github.ts:17). The bare `execFileSync` grep hit its 50-match cap and is dominated by docs/, so it cannot re-derive a count — but the two cited sites are confirmed present, which is all the claim needs: a non-test synchronous execFileSync precedent exists inside src/workflow/.","action":"none — verified sound, though note the probe was weak. The claim is existential ('a precedent exists'), not a count, so two confirmed anchors settle it; a future probe for this should grep `from 'node:child_process'` scoped to src/workflow/ rather than the bare symbol.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q4","ref":"t8 / c21","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"THE PLAN'S MOST CONSEQUENTIAL NEW CLAIM. A committed BUILD record exists with meta.standalone true and NO sizeClass key at all, carrying a plan-driven {commit, tasks} body. It disproves the CONVERSE of review claim p1 (which established only that sizeClass appears exclusively on standalone records), so the converged renderer's sizeClass-keyed title has an undefined case in REAL stored data and t8 must define it rather than inherit today's 'undefined' heading.","evidence":"Confirmed — the counterexample record exists and reads as described. Worth recording one incidental fact the probe surfaced: the hash be8708a9cd20e286 is ALSO the epic hash of an unrelated Epic, 'add-insrc-icon-vs-code-activity-E20260926be8708a9', so the record is that Epic's S001 BUILD record rather than an orphan. That makes it a normal product of the shipped pipeline, which strengthens the claim: the sizeClass-absent state is something the system actually produces, not a hand-corrupted file.","action":"none — verified sound, and it remains the plan's most consequential new finding. t8's absent-sizeClass fallback and t2's third golden both rest on it.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q5","ref":"t8 / c21","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing standalone renderer interpolates meta.sizeClass directly into the title, which is the mechanism by which a sizeClass-absent record renders a heading containing the literal string 'undefined' today.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim and uniquely: standalone-record.ts:116 is `` `# Build (standalone ${rec.meta.sizeClass}) — Story ${rec.meta.storyId}` `` — exactly one src/ match. A bare template interpolation of a possibly-absent field, so an absent sizeClass stringifies to the literal 'undefined' inside the heading. The mechanism behind the defect is established, not inferred.","action":"none — verified sound. Combined with q4 this makes the 'undefined' title an observed consequence of real stored data rather than a hypothetical.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q6","ref":"t8 / c23","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"sizeClass is REQUIRED on StandaloneBuildRecord.meta but OPTIONAL on BuildRecord.meta — so the counterexample record violates its own declared type, and the converged renderer (which takes a BuildRecord) can legitimately read an absent sizeClass. The code-review subject separately types buildRecord as StandaloneBuildRecord | null, which is why the type is retained rather than deleted.","evidence":"The two cited declarations are confirmed by their read anchors, and the asymmetry the plan relies on holds. But the grep surfaced a THIRD declaration the plan does not name: src/mcp/build-step/render.ts:137 `readonly sizeClass: string;` (required). So sizeClass is declared in THREE places across src/, not the two the plan accounts for.","action":"Record it in the plan rather than leave it latent. The third declaration does not change t8's design — the converged renderer takes a BuildRecord, whose sizeClass is optional, which is what makes the absent case legal to handle — but a task that touches the sizeClass contract should know there are three declarations in play, because a future reader reconciling them will otherwise find a site the plan never mentions. Add it to t8's summary as a one-line note alongside the :49/:94 pair.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The plan enumerates two sizeClass declarations as though they were exhaustive; a third exists in the build-step render types. The design is unaffected but the inventory is incomplete, and this Story is specifically about records that fail to state what they contain."}},{"claimId":"q7","ref":"t3 / c18","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The collector's never-throw catch AND its log.warn ALREADY exist, which reframes the fourth resolution's log-visibility requirement from new work into a preserve-and-assert regression guard. collectBuildChangeLog catches, logs 'could not derive the changed set; returning an empty change-log', and returns [].","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim. changed-files.ts:67 declares collectBuildChangeLog and :80 is the log line 'collectBuildChangeLog: could not derive the changed set; returning an empty change-log', with the catch at :77 and the `return []` at :82 both resolving. So ac6's never-throw AND the log-visibility requirement of resolution q378acc65 are ALREADY satisfied in the shipped code.","action":"none — verified sound, and the reframing matters: t3's job here is a regression guard over existing behaviour, not new work. That is why t3's acceptance check demands a seam that THROWS and asserts both the [] return and the log call, rather than merely asserting the return.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q8","ref":"t3 / c1","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"changedFiles today takes ONLY repoPath and derives strictly from the working tree via a staged/unstaged loop, with no commit-range path — which is what makes t3's options argument purely additive rather than a signature change.","evidence":"Confirmed on both anchors, each a single src/ hit: changed-files.ts:43 `export async function changedFiles(repoPath: string): Promise<readonly string[]> {` and :46 `for (const staged of [false, true])`. The function takes only repoPath and loops staged/unstaged, with no commit-range path — so t3's options argument is genuinely additive rather than a signature change.","action":"none — verified sound.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q9","ref":"t8 / c19","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The rename surface is LARGER than the LLD's 'two call sites': renderPlanBuildRecordMd has its declaration plus exactly ONE production call site, and roughly seventeen further references confined to two committed test suites — which is why t8 must RUN those suites rather than assume compilation suffices.","evidence":"Both read anchors resolve (standalone-record.ts:132 declaration, :200 the single production call). The symbol grep hit its 50-match cap and is dominated by docs/, so it could not re-derive the ~17 count from this probe alone — but a direct scoped enumeration run earlier in this session returned exactly the claimed shape: 5 src/ hits total (declaration, the :183 doc reference, the :199/:200 dispatch) plus the import and uses in build-record-enrichment.test.ts and provenance-build.test.ts.","action":"none — verified sound. The operative part of the claim is not the exact number but that the references span two committed test SUITES, which is what makes t8's 'RUN GREEN, not merely compiled' check necessary; that part is confirmed by the file names in the matches.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q10","ref":"t2 / c20","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"build-record.test.ts has exactly one external call to renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd, and it asserts the WRITTEN md equals that function's return value — the assertion that becomes a tautology once t8 makes the function a delegating shim, because persistBuildRecord then writes through the same converged renderer the shim resolves to. This is why t2 must convert it to a frozen golden.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim, and it is the single most important thing this review had to check. build-record.test.ts:142 is exactly `assert.equal(readFileSync(md, 'utf8'), renderStandaloneBuildRecordMd(rec));` — one src/ match. Written md on the left, the function's return on the right. Once t8 makes that function delegate to the converged renderer, persistBuildRecord writes through the same renderer, so both operands are the same function applied to the same record and the assertion holds for ANY output.","action":"none — verified sound. t2's conversion to a frozen golden, with the mutation proof that the pre-conversion form stays GREEN under a heading change, is the right remedy and is correctly ordered before t8.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q11","ref":"t2 / c20","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"provenance-build.test.ts:105 is a DIFFERENT and legitimate pattern that t2 must leave untouched — it compares the renderer against itself on two distinct inputs (a legacy record vs a base record), which is an equivalence check rather than the vacuous self-comparison being fixed.","evidence":"The read anchor at provenance-build.test.ts:105 resolves. Verified directly earlier in session: it is `assert.equal(renderPlanBuildRecordMd(legacy), renderPlanBuildRecordMd(baseRec));` — the same renderer applied to two DIFFERENT inputs, pinning that a legacy-shaped record renders identically to a base one. That is a genuine equivalence property, not a self-comparison, so it must be left alone.","action":"none — verified sound. Keeping this distinction explicit in the plan is worth the words: a sweep that 'fixed all renderer-against-renderer assertions' would destroy a legitimate legacy-compatibility guard while chasing the vacuous one.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q12","ref":"t8 / c4","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"The converged renderer's section list is nine members, of which exactly TWO always render (the title and the created/updated line) and SEVEN are conditional omit-slots: Commit, Scope, Triage rationale, Summary, Tasks validated, Changes, Feedback. The LLD's test strategy says 'eight omit-slots', which matches neither count — so t8 asserts against the seven actually counted.","evidence":"All seven conditional section literals resolve to real emit sites in standalone-record.ts, confirmed by a direct scoped read: '## Scope' :120, '## Triage rationale' :124, `**Commit:**` :140, '## Summary' :148, '## Tasks validated' :152, '## Changes' :163, '## Feedback' :167 (the last two each inside an `if` guard, visible at :163 and :167 as `lines.push('', '## Changes', ...)` / `lines.push('', '## Feedback', ...)`). Seven conditional plus the two unconditional (title, created/updated) gives nine, so the plan's recount is right and the LLD's 'eight omit-slots' is wrong.","action":"none — verified sound. The plan correctly refuses to inherit the LLD's unverified number and states the count it actually found, which is the behaviour the s6 audit asked for.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q13","ref":"t6 / c11","kind":"external-contract","severity":"LOW","premise":"The git_diff builtin already accepts a `from` range input, so t6's range derivation needs no new tool capability — only a different call. If this were false, the chosen approach would require new plumbing and t6's sizing would be wrong.","evidence":"The read anchors at diff.ts:55 and :62 resolve, confirming the `from` range input on the git_diff builtin. (The phrase grep returned four hits whose visible top three were docs/ citations of this same fact, so the probe was weak, but the typed read settles it — and the same anchor was confirmed verbatim during the LLD review as claim p4.)","action":"none — verified sound. t6 needs no new tool capability, only a different call, so its L sizing is driven by the fallback machinery rather than by tool work.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q14","ref":"t6 / c12","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The code-review grounding chain really does fall back to HEAD^ and then to git's empty-tree object — the shipped precedent t6 deliberately DECLINES to adopt, on the ground that a populated wrong answer is worse than an honest absence. The rejection is of a real mechanism, not a hypothetical one.","evidence":"Confirmed verbatim on both anchors, each a single src/ hit: grounding.ts:167 `const EMPTY_TREE_HASH = '4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904';` and :298 `return await runDiff(repoPath, { from: EMPTY_TREE_HASH });`. The fallback chain t6 declines to adopt is live shipped code, so the rejection is a real decision about a real mechanism.","action":"none — verified sound. This is what makes t6's 'a CLEAN tree with NO base yields an EMPTY set, NOT the last commit's files' check meaningful: it pins a deliberate divergence from a pattern that exists elsewhere in this very codebase and would otherwise look like the obvious thing to do.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q15","ref":"t1 / c13","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The gated throwaway-repo idiom t1 and every git-dependent integration test follow is committed and real: a git-availability gate that returns early, and read-only verification afterwards via rev-list --count and status --porcelain.","evidence":"Both read anchors resolve (diff-grounding.test.ts:150 and :175). Verified directly earlier in session: :150 is `try { execFileSync('git', ['--version'], { stdio: 'ignore' }); } catch { git = false; }` followed by `if (!git) { return; }`, and the read-only verification compares `rev-list --count` before/after (:165, :173) and asserts `status --porcelain` is empty (:175). The probe's `status', '--porcelain` grep found other sites (maintenance.ts:97, migrate-docs-tree.test.ts:193) rather than this one in its visible slice, so the read is what settles it.","action":"none — verified sound. The idiom t1 extends is committed and includes the read-only assertions, which t1's fourth acceptance check reproduces.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q16","ref":"t7 / c2, c8, c10","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"NONE of the three BUILD-record body writers sets summary or commit — the premise that makes both rendered fields dead branches and gives t7 its purpose. The three writers are the validate phase, the implement phase, and the completion-time writer.","evidence":"Confirmed by enumeration, and the decisive probe is the negative one: the `commit:` grep returned 8 src/ hits and NOT ONE is in a BUILD-record body writer — they are a CR table row, a Kotlin test field, an analyze comment and similar. All three body-writer anchors resolve (validate.ts:164, implement.ts:139, completion-record.ts:44). So summary and commit genuinely have zero producers, which is t7's entire premise.","action":"none — verified sound. Note the asymmetry this leaves: three tracked records nonetheless CARRY body.commit, which can only have been hand-written. t7's acceptance check already pins the premise from that direction.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q17","ref":"t9 / c14","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"runValidateSession is SHARED by both the plan-driven and standalone validate branches by design and writes `standalone: false` unconditionally — the live defect t9 removes. Its own comment states it serves both branches so the persist behaviour is identical.","evidence":"Confirmed on both anchors. validate.ts:121 carries the comment stating runValidateSession serves 'BOTH the plan-driven and the standalone' branches so the persist behaviour is identical, and :163 is inside it writing `standalone: false`. The shared-by-design property is stated by the code's own comment rather than inferred, which is what makes the flip a live defect rather than a theoretical path.","action":"none — verified sound. t9's removal targets a confirmed unconditional write on a confirmed shared path.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q18","ref":"s2 / t2","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The real pre-fix golden fixtures t2 requires are AVAILABLE in the tracked artifact tree rather than needing construction: BUILD-1d3bd81c5560de0d-s1 and BUILD-dcbb529e465417af-s1 both carry standalone true with exactly the pre-fix {focus, producesLld} body. Without these t2 would silently degrade into the constructed fixture it exists to avoid.","evidence":"Both fixture read anchors resolve. The `producesLld` grep hit its 50-match cap on docs/ and could not re-derive the body shape, but a direct enumeration earlier in this session parsed all 63 tracked BUILD records and found exactly five with standalone true, of which BUILD-1d3bd81c5560de0d-s1 and BUILD-dcbb529e465417af-s1 have body keys exactly ['focus', 'producesLld'] — the pre-fix shape t2 needs.","action":"none — verified sound. This closed a real risk: had no such record existed, t2 would have quietly degraded into the constructed fixture it exists to avoid, and its byte-identity guard would have agreed with the fix by construction.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q19","ref":"s2 ordering","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"The plan's two load-bearing orderings hold in the emitted task graph: t2 (order 2) strictly precedes t8 (order 8), so the byte-identity goldens are captured BEFORE the convergence that would otherwise record post-fix bytes; and t1 (order 1) precedes t6, t8 and t9, each of which inverts one of its three characterisations and declares t1 in dependsOn.","evidence":"The PLAN read anchor resolves and the orderings hold in the emitted artifact: t2 is order 2 and t8 is order 8 with t2 in t8's dependsOn; t1 is order 1 and appears in the dependsOn of t6, t8 and t9, each of which inverts one of its three named characterisations. Every dependency has a strictly lower order than its dependent, so the graph is acyclic and the order is a valid topological sort.","action":"none — verified sound. The golden-before-convergence ordering is the one the Story's byte-identity guarantee depends on, and it survived both the critique and the finalize step.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"q20","ref":"t6 sizing","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"The PLAN-introducing-commit fallback t6 must build has NO existing implementation to reuse — no `--diff-filter=A` lookup exists anywhere in src/ — which is the evidence behind the s3 critique that re-sized t6 from M to L so the build budgets for the lookup machinery instead of discovering it mid-task.","evidence":"The claim is WRONG as worded. I asserted that 'no --diff-filter lookup exists anywhere in src/'. FOUR exist: cherry-pick.ts:114, merge.ts:144, rebase.ts:148 and revert.ts:118, each `await runShell(['git', 'diff', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=U'], { cwd, timeoutMs: 10_000 })`. Confirmed by an independent scoped grep, not just the probe. What IS true is the narrower statement: `--diff-filter=A` appears nowhere in src/ (one hit, in the PLAN's own prose), so there is no existing 'which commit ADDED this file' lookup to reuse. The four existing sites are all `=U` for unmerged-conflict detection — a different question.","action":"Two corrections, and the second is more valuable than the first. (1) Reword t6's sizing rationale to the claim the evidence supports: no `--diff-filter=A` / added-file lookup exists, so the PLAN-commit resolver has to be written; the L sizing stands unchanged. (2) The four sites reveal something t6 should be told explicitly: they are async `runShell` calls in the DAEMON TOOL layer, and t6's resolver — unlike t5's stamp — runs inside collectBuildChangeLog's already-async call path, so t6 MAY use that async tool layer and should follow those four sites' argv-array idiom. t5 must be synchronous because gates.ts is; t6 need not be. The plan currently carries the sync constraint prominently for t5 and says nothing for t6, which invites the build to copy t5's execFileSync approach into a context that does not require it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"An overstated absence claim, where the correct narrower claim preserves the conclusion. Correcting it also surfaces the sync-vs-async split between t5 and t6, which the plan does not currently state and which determines where t6's resolver can live."}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":1,"low":19},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-02T14:16:31.843Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{"q20":{"findingId":"q20","status":"resolved","note":"Resolved by correcting t6 in the PLAN by hand (the finding carried a rationale but no artifactEdits). The claim was overstated: I asserted no --diff-filter lookup existed anywhere in src/. FOUR exist — cherry-pick.ts:114, merge.ts:144, rebase.ts:148, revert.ts:118 — each `await runShell(['git','diff','--name-only','--diff-filter=U'])`. The narrower claim the evidence supports is that `--diff-filter=A` appears nowhere in src/, so there is no added-file (\"which commit introduced this path\") lookup to reuse. t6 keeps its L sizing unchanged — the resolver still has to be written. The more valuable half of the correction is the second one: those four sites are ASYNC runShell calls in the daemon tool layer, and t6's resolver runs inside collectBuildChangeLog's already-async call path, so t6 is NOT constrained to a synchronous implementation the way t5 is. The plan carried the sync constraint prominently for t5 (gates.ts is sync) and said nothing for t6, which invited the build to copy t5's execFileSync approach into a context that does not require it. t6 now states it may use the async tool layer and should follow those sites' argv-array runShell idiom. Also applied the LOW q6 note in the same pass: sizeClass is declared in THREE places (adding src/mcp/build-step/render.ts:137), not the two t8 named. Design unaffected; recorded so the inventory is complete.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-02T14:17:27.266Z"}}}}}}
